<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN is deliberately NOT declared with <uses-permission>.

        It is a signature-level permission that is only ever granted to system apps, so
        a <uses-permission> entry would grant this app nothing at all — Lint rejects it
        as ProtectedPermissions, and it would still be a no-op if Lint were silenced.

        What actually protects the admin receiver is the android:permission attribute on
        that receiver below, which the system enforces at bind time regardless of any
        declaration here.
    -->

    <!--
        android:testOnly="true" must be present BEFORE `dpm set-device-owner` runs.

        Android refuses `dpm remove-active-admin` for any admin whose application did
        not declare itself test-only, so without this attribute the module becomes a
        Device Owner that can be cleared only by factory-resetting the device.

        The timing was proved by failure on an Android 11 bench device. A build that
        was made device owner WITHOUT this flag was later rebuilt WITH it and installed
        over the top (the package reports TEST_ONLY, confirmed by dumpsys). Every
        removal method was then retried and every one still refused:

          - plain uninstall for user 0  -> DELETE_FAILED_DEVICE_POLICY_MANAGER
          - disable the package         -> "Cannot disable a protected package"
          - clear the package data      -> "Cannot clear data for a protected package"
          - release the active admin    -> "Attempt to remove non-test admin"

        The reason is that the system stores the test-only answer at grant time and
        never re-reads it. Retrofitting the flag onto an existing grant does nothing.
        Only a factory reset removed it.

        So this attribute is a precondition, not a repair. Declared here it does two
        things a proof-of-concept should do:
          - it makes the admin releasable with `dpm remove-active-admin`, provided it
            was set as device owner after this build, so the device can be left exactly
            as it was found with no wipe and no residue; and
          - it makes the APK uninstallable through normal means, so it cannot be
            mistaken for or installed as a production component.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:label="CYVRA Sanitization"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The device-admin component. Guarded by BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN, so it can only be
            driven by the system that binds it.

            android:exported="true" is mandatory rather than discretionary: the system
            binds this receiver from outside the app, and targetSdk 36 refuses to build
            any intent-filter component that leaves android:exported unspecified. The
            BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN guard is what keeps "exported" from meaning "open" — only
            the system holds that signature-level permission.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".SanitizationAdmin"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_DEVICE_ADMIN">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.device_admin"
                android:resource="@xml/device_admin" />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.app.action.DEVICE_ADMIN_ENABLED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
            PROOF OF CONCEPT — deliberately exported and deliberately unguarded.

            This receiver must be exported and unguarded for the commissioned proof to
            work at all: the human operator drives it from `adb shell am broadcast`,
            and the shell UID would not satisfy a custom signature permission reliably
            across OEM builds.

            That also means ANY app on the device can send this intent and factory-reset
            it. This is acceptable only because the APK is a sacrificial proof artifact
            that is erased by its own action. It must not be shipped. See README.md.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".SanitizationBroadcastReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.cyvra.sanitization.ARM" />
                <action android:name="com.cyvra.sanitization.WIPE" />
                <action android:name="com.cyvra.sanitization.DISARM" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
